🤖 The [contributor README](https://github.com/marin-community/marin/blob/main/infra/marina/applets/snowball_family_tree/README.md) already calls for a focused PR with source and evidence links; the [maintenance guide](https://github.com/marin-community/marin/blob/main/docs/references/snowball-family-tree.md#maintain-the-applet) already calls for releases from a clean checkout at current remote main. The open decisions are how to accept outside additions and when to release.

I recommend reviewed PRs for outside contributions, including PRs opened on a contributor's behalf. Batch releases after related tree PRs merge; release promptly when a PR corrects public lineage or its content is needed sooner. Each tree PR should say whether it needs publication, and the release record should identify the published version and main commit.

@penfever, please confirm the PR requirement and release cadence. Once confirmed, update both guides with that policy and apply it to the planned SFT shootout batch (`model: null` for runs without public weights). The issue reports that v6 graph data matches main, so no graph correction is identified here.

🤖 Screening 30,000 NuminaMath-CoT answers surfaced three more ways math grading fails beyond the chain-length crash:

- Clock times. `10:00`, `18\!:\!00` and `1:00 \text{ AM}` parse as division by zero, and math-verify raises `Can't evaluate nan or zoo`. That was 6 of the 7 crashes in the sample.
- Unit stripping. math-verify's unit normalization deletes a trailing spaced single letter as a unit, so `p < q < r < s` normalizes to `p < q < r <` and `-m > n > -n > m` fails to parse. `a<b<c<d`, with no spaces, survives.
- `\neq`. math-verify's relation table has no entry for `Unequality`.

Also, a chain parses as a SymPy conjunction, which deduplicates equivalent relations: `m < -n < n < -m` keeps two, since `n < -m` restates `m < -n`. So two equivalent chains can reach math-verify's in-order strict `zip` with different lengths.

The fix in progress handles each case:
- chains compare as sets of relations;
- clock-time references compare as times;
- `a \neq b` compares as `a = b`;
- a parse whose normalized text ends in an operator is redone with unit stripping off;
- an undefined reference raises `InvalidTask`;
- any other math-verify comparison error raises `GradingInfraError` rather than scoring 0.

With it, the 30,000-row screen has no crashes.
